Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 15:29:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix a use-after-free in the peephole optimiser, and find
 bmaps without an assign

Both found by building a real multi-module project (submods/list) through
a generated Makefile rather than through bas.

change() (sym.c) freed a CODE node's fields before copying the new ones in,
but callers routinely pass one of that node's OWN fields -- opt.c's
extb_for_020() passes first->srcopr, addq_for_add() passes first->srcopr
and first->destopr.  alloc_code() keeps opcode, srcopr and destopr in a
single allocation, so the string being read had already been freed.  With
-O, list.b came out with "extb.l <garbage>" and vasm rejected it.  This is
a pre-existing bug, reproducible through plain "bas -SO"; it is not
specific to the new build path.  change() now takes a private copy first.

make_bmap_name() only knew $ACE_BMAPS and the ACEbmaps: assign, so ace
could not find a .bmap when invoked from a Makefile with neither -- and
aide_makegen.h promises a "complete, self-standing" Makefile.  It now also
looks at <dir of ace>/../sdk/ACE/bmaps, derived from argv[0], which is
exactly the installed cross-compiler layout.  Candidates are tried in
order and only accepted if the file is really there, so the assign still
wins on a native Amiga.

ace also gains -0 to force 68000.  Until now the only way to turn 68020
code generation off was OPTION 2- in the source, which a build system
configuring the CPU per project cannot use.

Verified: all 223 non-error cases build both with and without -O (they
were only ever built without it before), 12/12 yap tests, amiga-check
still builds, and compare-host-vs-amiga.sh still reports the same 192
identical .s files.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
